hi everybody i'm margaret smith i'm the  corn breeder here in plant breeding and  genetics at cornell university  we have a very applied field corn  breeding program and a tiny bit of sweet  corn breeding that we do so i'd just  like to tell you about our program in  general and then a few specific  illustrations since our time is short of  some of the things we're working on  right now  new york state is a state where dairy is  the primary agricultural enterprise and  one of the primary feedstuffs for that  dairy is corn both grain and silage  so field corn is one of the most  important crops in the state it occupies  about a million acres of land here in  new york  and we try to breed field corn in my  program for what i would call niches so  new york state is a bit of a niche  compared to where most of the corn  production in the u.s is because we have  a shorter growing season we tend to have  weather like this not in july but this  year it's in july wearing a coat in the  field in july is a really unusual event  but in our in the fall particularly our  weather tends to be very cool and wet so  we have some unique challenges during  the harvest season that you don't see in  the main parts of the central corn belt  and in the spring our ground tends to be  very wet so we folk we need varieties  which will work with cool wet soil  conditions in the spring and lots of  cloudy wet weather in the fall so that's  some of what makes new york state corn a  niche  another niche we work on is organics we  have a lot of organic growers and the  organic average is increasing so we do a  bit of reading work for organics  and then the other thing that i do to  complement both of those is a lot of  work on developing sources of resistance  to diseases and insects  so that's the kind of overview of some  of our major breeding objectives  we have a breeding nursery site here in  aurora new york which is about 35 miles  north of ithaca it's a major corn  growing part of the state if i were to  grow my breeding nursery near campus  not only is it a little even shorter  season than what we have to deal with  here so there are a lot of things i  couldn't effectively cross or work with  because the growing season is too short  there  but also it's not a major corn growing  area so i get every deer raccoon student  needing a decoration for their porch and  everything else taking a big toll on my  field so we grew our corn up here in  keuka county which is one of the larger  dairy and field crops producing counties  in new york state surrounded by lots of  acres of fields porn to dilute out all  of those  deer and raccoon and bird problems and  so on that we otherwise would have to  deal with  so our breeding nursery is here at  aurora  in addition to the breeding program we  have a variety testing program  that involves sites  seven conventional on-farm sites two  organic sites  and those are all focused on grain  testing corn testing for grain  we also do both commercial commercial  corn testing for grain as well as for  silage so we have four additional silage  testing sites that we run here in new  york state together with a team of folks  from animal science and in collaboration  with colleagues in vermont so we have a  vermont new york corn silage testing  program which is nice because it picks  up on a lot of the similarities of those  growing environments and the fact that  vermont also is a very big dairy state  so it gives us a broader sampling of  sites a nice collaboration that we can  do for the commercial variety testing  between those two locations  so that's the quick overview of the  scope of our program  um i want to start by talking about a  little of the work we've been doing on  organic corn breeding  and it's always unclear what makes a  variety perform better in organic  conditions than another  it seems fairly apparent that not  necessarily  the best varieties in conventional  growing systems will always be the best  for organic growing systems and you can  imagine a lot of the conditions that  would make that true the organic growers  tend to plant later because they're  having a  legume or some other kind of cover crop  plow down before they can get in the  fields  they plant without commercial synthetic  seed treatment chemicals so germination  and establishment is really important  they  do a lot of aggressive weak control so  you have to have a variety that will  withstand that but translating that kind  of thing those those  um speculations as to traits that  nitrogen would be another one nitrogen  availability is a more gradual process  compared to synthetic nitrogen which you  know you pour on  some is a starter and a bunch is a side  dress it comes in big flushes for the  organic corn nitrogen is a more gradual  slow to start in the spring  stretched out over the season  availability  so you can imagine a lot of things that  would cause a variety to be better  adapted to that than to conventional and  vice versa but most of those are  speculative and they're hard to  translate into specific traits that i'm  going to look for as i go through my  nursery and say i'm selecting for this  because that will make the better  organic corn  so we do a lot of our selection for  organics based on variety evaluation  organic systems looking at what works  well trying to make new combinations  among some of the best performing things  and re-selecting those sort of standard  approach to trying to improve adaptation  and yield performance in a corn breeding  program  figure out what works try and combine  things that work well to get something  even better  one of the things we've done is a little  unique in our organic  breeding effort is  i worked with colleagues kevin  montgomery out in illinois and a  colleague of his to do cold stress  testing on seeds and seedlings so i  mentioned that we have cold wet soils in  the spring that organic those fields  need to be able to establish and 