 on and we just like to compare  whether they're going to really help us  because the cold stress test effort as  you might imagine distressing seeds by  pedigree and putting them in germination  plates and hand transplanting them to  the field is a fairly labor intensive  process so we'll see whether that's  likely to work or not  another area i'd like to talk about is  our disease and insect resistance  breeding so we do this program  historically has done a lot of breeding  for resistance to various corn tests  and it fits well into our adaptation  both for conventional and organic so it  feeds into those programs and all the  progenies that come out of it go right  into our testing program  so i want to take a few moments to talk  about several of the different pest  resistance efforts that we have ongoing  one of the diseases we've worked a lot  with is the anthracnose stock rot and  the progenies here on my left are some  of the results of some of that selection  so anthracnose stockrot is a  atypical stock rotting organism most of  the stock rots in corn attack the stock  when it's senescing so the plant is  already on the way out the the tissue is  dying the rot moves into that dying  tissue so we call them stock rots of  senescence anthracnose is not like that  it actually attacks stocks when they're  still healthy and vibrant so at around  flowering time or a little before  and what that means is that stocks are  also critically important still in terms  of translocation of water and  photosynthates during that time period  so the fact that the rod is attacking  and moving through the stalk in that  early time in the plant's development  means that it has much more potential to  cause  yield losses you know it's cutting off  photosynthetic transportation to the ear  it's restricting movement of water and  nutrients up from the soil so it can be  really problematic in addition to  weakening the stock and you may remember  at the beginning i said we have all that  cloudy wet weather in the fall which  means our plants have to stay standing  in the field oftentimes longer than  farmers would wish sometimes  they're just not maturing very fast  because of the cool cloudy wet weather  sometimes they're mature enough but the  farmer can't get near them to harvest  because the ground is too wet so stock  quality and standability despite that  are really important to our growers out  here in the northeast  so we've worked on selecting for  resistance to anthrax stockrot i have a  notable memory from speaking about this  to a seed industry meeting and having  one of the industry guys raise his hand  and say margaret you know that all looks  really exciting great resistance levels  but make sure you're not selecting for  resistance to yield too because this is  what often happens we can develop really  resistant things but they're really  resistant maybe because they aren't even  growing an ear there's nothing drawing  the nutrients out of the stock to begin  with or just because they seem to take a  big yield hit so our techno stockrot  breeding program we have run by doing  every generation along the way we'll  take our selected progenies that appear  to have stock rot resistance cross them  to a stock susceptible tester to  evaluate that resistance in a hybrid  combination to make sure they're  transferring resistance to their hybrid  progeny and simultaneously cross that  line to a good yield tester so that we  can look at those hybrids and make sure  we're not breeding for resistance to  yield  so the proteins on my left here are some  of the ones that have come out of an  effort that started in the germplasm  enhancement of maize program which was a  federally funded effort  to  enhance the diversity in commercial maze  germplasm it was a collaborative effort  between the maze the corn seed industry  and the public sector breeders and a  variety of hubs where the the gem or  germplasm enhancement of may's program  was centered so we got some funding  early on to work with that we identified  progenies the one that i'm standing next  to happens to have come from a variety  that came from chile but  varieties that had come from the  germplasm collections largely from the  tropics and subtropics that are hugely  diverse in maize but have not been  widely used in commercial breeding  so these are some of the things emerging  from that breeding effort for  anthracnose stock rot the resistance is  looking really good we will inject these  right into the base of the stock with  stock rot and split the stocks at  harvest to evaluate how far the stock  rifles traveled up the stocks make sure  we're selecting for resistance and then  in the background behind me the stuff  that's really tall you can see are some  of the test cross hybrids that are being  evaluated for anthracnose stock rot  resistance so as i mentioned each  generation of the way s2s s3s s4s we are  crossing simultaneously to both a rod  susceptible tester to evaluate the  contribution of this progeny to the  stock rod of the hybrid and that's the  hybrid evaluation going on behind me and  at the same time  a yield potential tester to evaluate its  contribution and adequacy and adaptation  for yield and productivity here in new  york  so that's some of our anthracnose  stockrot work  one of the other things that acts  synergistically with the anthracnose rot  is the european corn borer  there is less of a problem with that now  because of the widespread use of bt corn  but there are still a number of  circumstances where growers may not want  to use or be able to use the bt as a  control mechanism for european corn  borer so you can imagine organic growers  are not using those things  and growers who are planting bt also  need refuge acres that are non-bt  thirdly i maintain that having some  innate corn based resistance in the  background of bt will probably help to  ensure the longevity and the  effectiveness over time the durability  of that bt-based resistance so for all  those reasons we've continued to work on  european corn borer resistance and the  european corn borer has been shown to  act synergistically with the anthracnose  stock rot so if you have corn borers  creating holes in the stock you have  entry points for the rot  there is some evidence that the corn  borers can even vector in the  anthracnose anthracnose will cause a  leaf light disease phase which can  produce spores that can then be carried  in by the insects  incidentally resistance to the leaf  flight phase of the anthracnose disease  is genetically unrelated to resistance  to the stuffrt phase so that makes  selection particularly challenging  but the corn borer can act  synergistically so we've had a  long-standing relationship  with several international programs that  select for borah resistance most notably  the one at the international maze and  wheat improvement center symmet in  mexico  summit developed a multiple border  resistant population  that was selected all across the world  including here in new york for  resistance to an a large array of  different types of borers most of the  tropical and sub-tropical borers are  much more aggressive than the european  corn borer that we primarily deal with  here so having things coming out of that  program provided really strong levels of  resistance to stock boring by the  european corn border this is another  another pest where the leaf damage phase  and resistance to it is genetically  unrelated to resistance to the stock  boring phase so these are the two  classic problems you can select for leaf  feeding resistance but it does nothing  necessarily for stock boring resistance  and as you might imagine  stock boring damage by this insect has  the potential to cause much more yield  loss you've got tunnels through the  stock all the same reasons i talked  about for the anthracnose stock rod  tunnels that cut off translocation  through the stock stock has weakened and  fall over for all of the same reasons  so we took some of these materials from  the multiple bore resistant population  in mexico and also the one that my  former colleague who i miss very much  john mim the entomologist at simmet for  many years  deemed the  brg  um if you worked with john at the time  you would know that brg stands for  bordeaux resistant gringo  so he he called it that because he had  left it from u.s germplasm that's the  gringo part  adjacent to the multiple bore resistant  population and he harvested a bunch of  top crosses off of that so it was  multiple bore resistant by us corn belt  germplasm so that's the brg so this line  right to my right is one of our  progenies that is emerging from that  breeding effort with the brg  we are again selecting for insect  resistance every step of the way as we  inbreed these making test crosses both  to susceptible testers to evaluate  whether the inbred conveys that  resistance to its hybrid progeny and to  commercial testers to evaluate its yield  potential and adaptation and general  productivity  interestingly enough we were struggling  and struggling with crosses to this this  materials  derived from the brg we kept having poor  seat set and i was stewing for a couple  years about what was going wrong with  that and finally the light went off and  i think they have a gametophyte factor a  lot of tropical genetic materials in  corn have floating around in them  gametophyte factors which limit what can  be crossed to it what will pollinate it  successfully and i'm suspecting these  have a gametophyte factor floating  around in them so we're making some  controlled test crosses here in the  nursery this year to evaluate  whether that's actually the case i'm  thinking that's probably a ga1s this is  the same factor that's used in  the white corn industry and the popcorn  industry to prevent  lots of cross-pollination commercial  crops in those in those arenas with u.s  standard us number two yellow dent if  you're trying to grow white corn you  don't want lots of yellow kernels in  there if you're trying to grow popcorn  you have this gametophyte factor to  reduce the chances of  pollination from adjacent field corn  that would decrease the popping quality  of your crop  so we're evaluating those for whether  they do in fact carry this gametophyte  factor and we also have hybrid  evaluations both for yield as well as  for corn bore resistance going on with  some of these new sources from the  famous brg  so the last thing i want to talk about  today i just finished talking about  multiple insect resistance  and i'd like to talk for a moment about  multiple disease resistance  one of the things that's emerged from  our breeding program is a few inbreds to  a pair of inbred sister lines that we  had originally selected for resistance  to gray leaf spot which is one of the  more problematic leaf lights in corn  and upon further evaluation it turned  out that they in fact had a fairly broad  spectrum of disease resistance to not  only gray leaf spot but also northern  leaf blight some level of resistance to  southern leaf blight anthracnose leaf  blight rust and even stewart's wilt  which is a bacterial disease they had  some moderate resistance so we got  interested in the cause of that multiple  disease resistance one of my graduate  students did a fairly big  recombinant  back cross population to try and  understand the genetics underlying that  multiple disease resistance it involved  a lot of different disease evaluations  and paper should be coming out it was  just accepted so paper should be coming  out shortly on that  to cut to the chase he found a number of  different loci several of which are in  areas that have been identified as kind  of hot spots in the maze genome for  disease resistance one of the things  that emerged was the area that contains  the ligulus one gene which has also just  been published as having some  relationship to disease resistance  ligulus one has to do with the formation  of the  the ligul and the oracle and that  leaf blade sheath interface the whole  development pathway there but it also  appears to be linked through perhaps  liguillus ii to some regulatory systems  that may control the salicylic acid  pathway so that would be a logical  reason for it to be perhaps related to  disease resistance so we're still trying  to understand this rather serendipitous  multiple disease resistance we found  but one of the things i'm doing with it  is trying to back cross it into sweet  corn so you may have noticed that this  corn looks a little different from what  we were looking at this is sweet corn so  we are trying to do some generally slow  gradual back crossing to see if we can  move some of that multiple disease  resistance trait into sweet corn a lot  of sweet corns one of the primary things  that they need is a good disease  resistance package  that's the primary thing after the real  primary thing which is good flavor and  quality um so we're focused on how can  we piggyback some sweet corn improvement  in a small way on our large field corn  breeding program that's by far the  biggest part of my effort and so this is  one of the examples so the progenies i'm  standing in front of right now are a  bunch of progenies that have been  we're doing back crossing with selfie  generations in between and then we  screen those selfie generations for  several different diseases you can  imagine this takes a lot of field space  and a bit of time to get through several  diseases and especially when you know  you have the year where the anthracnose  doesn't do anything in the year where we  don't happen to have any rust or the  year where we have way too much of one  disease and you can't even see the rest  of them but we are slowly trying to move  some of these genes into a sweet corn  background  probably from our program that won't be  a commercial sweet corn that will be a  source material that the industry or  other sweet corn breeders could use in  their breeding efforts  so there's a quick lightning tour of  some of the major breeding objectives  we're working on in my program i hope  you've enjoyed it and i hope you have a  great time attending all of the other  talks that are part of this meeting  thanks very much  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

